A||||4||HarryG||urethane bushings
Z||000000||HarryG||09-02-99||09:49 AM||||I'm reading Herb Adams' book "Chassis Engineering" and have come to the chapter on Bushings and Deflections. Herb doesn't have a real high opinion of urethane suspension bushings. But he does say that they can be customized to work by hand-fitting to get proper clearances and installing grease fittings for regular lubing.  I've seen lots of discussions about these bushings, but mostly they dealt with install procedures. What about ongoing maintenance for a daily driver? Anyone installed grease fittings to lube them? BTW, has anyone used Herb Adams steel bushings for the rear control arms on 84-87's?      <br>Thanks!
Z||000001||Dale||09-02-99||10:38 AM||||Metal is great on the race track.  the urithane bushings are a comprimise in workability and comfort.  The problem with metal is you  feal every bump, vibration, pebble, etc.  The urithane is a hard suface so it doesn't have the play that rubber has, yet it is soft enough (barely) to absorb some of the vibrations etc.   They will greatly improve handling.
Z||000002||theogre||09-02-99||10:48 AM||||typically in race applications spherical joints are installed in the control arms. These joints are very high mantinance items. There are newer ones with teflon bearings in them that reduce the mantinance buy they still must be checked often for damage. I would definatly not put them in a daily drive.<p>There are also aluminum and steel cradle "bushing" sets. These could be installed in a daily driver without much problem. I quote bushings here because they really aren't bushings as far as I'm concerned. If you use one of these set you should probably install urethane dog bone bushings.<p>Read down thru the forum at some of the other bushing threads. There are a number of things that can be done to reduce squeek, and improve fit if needed.
Z||000003||batboy||09-02-99||11:02 AM||||The best polyurethane bushings are graphite impregnated bushings, but nobody makes them for Fiero that I could find.<p>Prothane brand are good bushings but are not the graphite ones. I was asking them about what I could do to eliminate the squeeking problem. The told me to use the proper sized tap and cut threads into the inner bushing holes. They also sent me some grease to use. The newly tapped grooves are supposed to retain the grease and keep the bushing lubricated. A washer goes over the end to keep the grease from oozing out around the bolt head.<p>I just got my new Prothane bushings today, but I'm still waiting on my front springs. I'll let you guys know if this works ok as soon as I round up all the rest of the parts: ball joints, tie rod ends, and Koni shocks.<p>Dale's right, metal bushings are the way to go if you are racing, but not for street use. Urethane bushings may not be perfect, but they're so much better than worn out factory rubber bushings.
Z||000004||lowCG||09-02-99||11:23 AM||||The best(street)rear bushings I could come up with were made out of UHMW,a plastic with very good compression characteristics.However,these took $30 worth of material,and a couple of hours standing over a lathe to produce,and I chose urethane this time around,to save time/hassle.<p>Batboy,IF(and only if)you got the VW lowering springs,I sincerely hope you got the RACING ones(Nuespeed#355.1005,or equivalent),as I found great disappointment in my installing the "sport" ones this past week;the car dropped(excessively).<IMG SRC="/ubb/frown.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15>  <br>Here I thought it would be easy,just because I'd done it once before.
